00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Travelling with Banjoko made it all feel different. 

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Of course he was not the first bird I met on my journey; migrants rarely get privacy. 

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
But since I left home, that was the first time I felt someone truly understood me. 

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:33.000
It was nice to have somebody to talk to, to laugh with, to discuss what we would do when we made it to Europe. 

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.000
We had so many plans. We both really wanted to go to England, we even joked about sharing a nest one day. 

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:51.000
It felt like we had always known each other. Hardship makes you bond faster, I guess. 

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Suddenly, I forgot why I had decided to leave home. After the Sahara, Europe was just so easy to enjoy. 

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000
We travelled short distances, taking the time to eat and rest. We crossed borders, always heading North. 

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.000
It was a beautiful spring and there was plenty of food everywhere. 

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
We felt happy. We felt safe. 

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Of course, we knew there were still dangers. 
We had heard the tales of other travellers, the stories of birds getting lost, starving to death, dying of diseases or being shot by hunters. 

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
But somehow, we believed this could not happen to us. 

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Because together, we felt invincible.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:41.000
And together we were. 
